the source through the Astrum Brief and Raven''s Lens, listen to or review the audio script, study the visual asset concept, then complete the linked mission task or AEGIS scenario response.","Mission Task Prompt":"Map trust boundaries and recommend a defensible assurance architecture.","Dash Deliverable":"Annotated diagram + 1-2 page assurance memo or control crosswalk.","Evaluation Focus":"Systems thinking, mission relevance, trust-and-assurance reasoning, decision communication, and evidence quality."},"research":{"Source ID":"M23","Research Vector":"Investigate how cybersecurity controls translate into measurable mission assurance outcomes in hybrid space systems.","Master''s-Level Option":"20-30 page applied research paper or design study linked to a weekly work plan and AEGIS scenario.","PhD / Publication Option":"Publication-oriented manuscript on the unresolved trust, assurance, architecture, or workforce issue surfaced by the source.","Cross-Links / Best-Fit Pathways":"Space Cybersecurity; Mission Assurance"}}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title=excluded.title, official_source=excluded.official_source, tier=excluded.tier, domain=excluded.domain, theme=excluded.theme, pathways=excluded.pathways, core_selective=excluded.core_selective, public_url=excluded.public_url, phase2=excluded.phase2, content=excluded.content, updated_at=now();
insert into public.sources (id,title,official_source,tier,domain,theme,pathways,core_selective,public_url,phase2,content) values ('M24','NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0','NIST','Tier 1 - Core Astrum Source','Cyber governance','Mission assurance and space systems','Space Cybersecurity; Mission Assurance','Core','https://www.nccoe.nist.gov/projects/hybrid-satellite-networks-cybersecurity',false,'{"brief":{"Source ID":"M24","Brief ID":"AB-M24","Proposed Brief Title":"NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0","Brief Abstract / Teaching Angle":"This brief turns a technical cybersecurity source into an Astrum mission-assurance asset. Instead of presenting controls in isolation, the brief links them to spacecraft, ground segment, hybrid networks, software provenance, identity, and third-party dependencies. The learning objective is to make students see cyber guidance as a means of preserving mission continuity and decision confidence.","Key Learning Payoff":"General cyber risk structure for mission systems.","What Changed / Why Now":"These frameworks are increasingly used as translational bridges between technical security practice and mission assurance requirements.","Suggested Dash Deliverable":"Annotated diagram + 1-2 page assurance memo or control crosswalk."},"lens":{"Source ID":"M24","Lens ID":"RL-M24","Raven''s Lens Commentary":"Astrum interprets this material through the language of trust boundaries. The key question is never just whether a control exists, but whether mission confidence survives when data cross organizational, software, orbital, terrestrial, and commercial boundaries.","Hidden Assumption":"That security controls alone guarantee mission confidence without a clear evidentiary model.","Likely Failure Point":"A mismatch between stated policy intent and the technical evidence available during real mission stress.","What They Didn''t Say":"Which controls matter most when mission continuity and operational decision quality—not just compliance—are the central goals.","Signature Raven Question":"Where must trust be established at the source, and where can it be inherited?"},"questions":{"Source ID":"M24","CQ-1":"What mission problem is this source really trying to solve?","CQ-2":"Where are the highest-value trust boundaries: identity, software, ground segment, or data path?","CQ-3":"What evidence would you want before acting on information from this system?","CQ-4":"Which failure would hurt the mission most even if the organization remained technically compliant?","CQ-5":"How would you convert this reading into a commander brief, exercise, or research question?"},"audio":{"Source ID":"M24","Audio ID":"AA-M24","Episode / Briefing Title":"NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0","Est. Duration":"6-8 min","Voice / Tone":"Documentary-intelligence / authoritative / calm","Opening Hook":"Cybersecurity becomes mission-critical when a single weak link can quietly distort the operational picture.","Segment 1":"Situation: define the mission context, the document type, and why it exists now.","Segment 2":"Core content: explain the architecture, framework, strategy, or doctrine in plain operational language.","Segment 3":"Astrum translation: identify the cyber, trust, AI, resilience, or command implications and end with a Raven question.","Closing Raven Question":"Where must trust be established at the source, and where can it be inherited?"},"visual":{"Source ID":"M24","Visual ID":"AV-M24","Asset Title":"Astrum Source Explainer","Recommended Format":"1-page visual brief","Key Story the Visual Should Tell":"Source purpose, system map, trust point, and one decision problem","Must-Show Elements":"A flexible format for any source.","Production Notes":"Use Astrum visual language: dark, precise, premium, and mission-focused."},"aegis":{"Source ID":"M24","Linked Inject IDs":"INJ-12","Suggested Assignment IDs":"A05, A06, A07, A08","Suggested Weekly Activity":"Read the source through the Astrum Brief and Raven''s Lens, listen to or review the audio script, study the visual asset concept, then complete the linked mission task or AEGIS scenario response.","Mission Task Prompt":"Map trust boundaries and recommend a defensible assurance architecture.","Dash Deliverable":"Annotated diagram + 1-2 page assurance memo or control crosswalk.","Evaluation Focus":"Systems thinking, mission relevance, trust-and-assurance reasoning, decision communication, and evidence quality."},"research":{"Source ID":"M24","Research Vector":"Identify a measurable trust, resilience, or mission-assurance gap implied by the source and frame it as a publishable research question.","Master''s-Level Option":"20-30 page applied research paper or design study linked to a weekly work plan and AEGIS scenario.","PhD / Publication Option":"Publication-oriented manuscript on the unresolved trust, assurance, architecture, or workforce issue surfaced by the source.","Cross-Links / Best-Fit Pathways":"Space Cybersecurity; Mission Assurance"}}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title=excluded.title, official_source=excluded.official_source, tier=excluded.tier, domain=excluded.domain, theme=excluded.theme, pathways=excluded.pathways, core_selective=excluded.core_selective, public_url=excluded.public_url, phase2=excluded.phase2, content=excluded.content, updated_at=now();
insert into public.sources (id,title,official_source,tier,domain,theme,pathways,core_selective,public_url,phase2,content) values ('M25','NIST AI Risk Management Framework 1.0','NIST','Tier 1 - Core Astrum Source','AI assurance','AI assurance, autonomy, and human judgment','Trusted AI & Autonomy','Core','https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework',true,'{"brief":{"Source ID":"M25","Brief ID":"AB-M25","Proposed Brief Title":"Translating AI Risk Management into Operational Trust","Brief Abstract / Teaching Angle":"This brief frames the source around a single Astrum question: what evidence should a human decision-maker require before trusting model output or autonomous behavior? Students should finish with a grounded understanding of AI lifecycle risk, guardrails, adversarial pressure, audit needs, and where human judgment remains indispensable in mission-critical systems.","Key Learning Payoff":"Govern, map, measure, manage AI risk.","What Changed / Why Now":"Decision-support and autonomous systems are becoming more central, increasing the value of auditable trust and human judgment frameworks.","Suggested Dash Deliverable":"AI assurance worksheet + decision memo or audit evidence package."},"lens":{"Source ID":"M25","Lens ID":"RL-M25","Raven''s Lens Commentary":"Astrum uses this source to separate performance from trust. A system can be accurate, fast, and still operationally untrustworthy if it cannot explain itself, resist manipulation, or preserve evidence of how a recommendation emerged.","Hidden Assumption":"That performance and trust will scale together unless explicitly managed.","Likely Failure Point":"Unclear thresholds for human override, weak evidence retention, or adversarial manipulation.","What They Didn''t Say":"What a commander should literally see before accepting a recommendation from a model or autonomous subsystem.","Signature Raven Question":"What evidence would make you trust—or reject—this machine-generated recommendation?"},"questions":{"Source ID":"M25","CQ-1":"What mission problem is this source really trying to solve?","CQ-2":"Which part of the human-machine relationship here is least mature?","CQ-3":"What evidence should a human see before trusting the model or autonomous action?","CQ-4":"Where could adversary pressure, ambiguity, or simple failure distort the picture?","CQ-5":"How would you convert this reading into a commander brief, exercise, or research question?"},"audio":{"Source ID":"M25","Audio ID":"AA-M25","Episode / Briefing Title":"Translating AI Risk Management into Operational Trust","Est. Duration":"6-8 min","Voice / Tone":"Documentary-intelligence / authoritative / calm","Opening Hook":"A fast answer from a machine is not the same thing as a trusted answer. This source helps us understand the difference.","Segment 1":"Situation: define the mission context, the document type, and why it exists now.","Segment 2":"Core content: explain the architecture, framework, strategy, or doctrine in plain operational language.","Segment 3":"Astrum translation: identify the cyber, trust, AI, resilience, or command implications and end with a Raven question.","Closing Raven Question":"What evidence would make you trust—or reject—this machine-generated recommendation?"},"visual":{"Source ID":"M25","Visual ID":"AV-M25","Asset Title":"Human–Machine Trust Boundary","Recommended Format":"Decision-flow diagram","Key Story the Visual Should Tell":"Inputs, model, guardrails, confidence, override, audit evidence","Must-Show Elements":"Show where human judgment enters and what evidence is visible.","Production Notes":"Use Astrum visual language: dark, precise, premium, and mission-focused."},"aegis":{"Source ID":"M25","Linked Inject IDs":"INJ-05, INJ-12","Suggested Assignment IDs":"A19, A20, A23","Suggested Weekly Activity":"Read the source through the Astrum Brief and Raven''s Lens, listen to or review the audio script, study the visual asset concept, then complete the linked mission task or AEGIS scenario response.","Mission Task Prompt":"Produce an AI assurance plan and defend the evidence required for operational trust.","Dash Deliverable":"AI assurance worksheet + decision memo or audit evidence package.","Evaluation Focus":"Systems thinking, mission relevance, trust-and-assurance reasoning, decision communication, and evidence quality."},"research":{"Source ID":"M25","Research Vector":"Examine explainability, auditability, and adversarial robustness requirements for AI or autonomous components in mission-critical space contexts.","Master''s-Level Option":"20-30 page applied research paper or design study linked to a weekly work plan and AEGIS scenario.","PhD / Publication Option":"Publication-oriented manuscript on the unresolved trust, assurance, architecture, or workforce issue surfaced by the source.","Cross-Links / Best-Fit Pathways":"Trusted AI & Autonomy"}}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title=excluded.title, official_source=excluded.official_source, tier=excluded.tier, domain=excluded.domain, theme=excluded.theme, pathways=excluded.pathways, core_selective=excluded.core_selective, public_url=excluded.public_url, phase2=excluded.phase2, content=excluded.content, updated_at=now();
